  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n    We employ a multi-resolution optimization with gradient projection to aggressively tackle the minimum overlap problem. Unlike previous methods that relied heavily on binary structures, we use a continuous optimization framework with adaptive grid refinement and targeted perturbations. The core idea is to iteratively improve the overlap integral by:\n    1. Applying gradient-based optimization at fixed grid resolutions\n    2. Transferring solutions to higher resolutions \n    3. Using function perturbations (jitter) to escape local optima\n    4. Maintaining a balanced exploration/exploitation strategy\n\n    Key differentiators:\n    - Reduces grid switching overhead by initializing higher grids directly\n    - Uses projected gradient descent with sophisticated bound handling\n    - Employs low-rank approximation of relevant gradients for efficiency\n    - Includes structured exploration phases (norm-bounded, target-focused jitter)\n\n    Mathematical specification:\n    - Representation: Piecewise constant function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] of length n\n    - Objective: Minimize max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n    - Constraints:\n        0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n        \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] = n/2 (normalized by dx=2/n)\n\n    Our approach:\n    1. Start from supplied grid n=360 if available\n    2. Use gradient projection (non-negativity handling)\n    3. For each candidate, compute gradient only for \"relevant\" lags to save computation\n    4. Project all while preserving normalization constraint\n\n    Implementation procedure:\n    1. Load parent (if present)\n    2. Process parent:\n        - Normalize: ensure \u2211h = n/2 and 0\u2264h\u22641\n        - Use SLSQP with adjusted gradient (for speed)\n    3. Define grid refinement path:\n        - n_grids = [360, 720, 1440] (dx quadrupling each time)\n    4. For each grid size:\n        a. Either:\n           i. Initialize from parent solution (if grid is a doubling)\n           ii. Use random initialization for new grid size\n        b. Run projected gradient optimization until:\n            - Convergence\n            - Time crunch\n            - Sufficient improvement unavailable\n        c. Apply jitter (added noise with normalization) to escape local optimum\n        d. Next grid: same orientation + perturbation handling\n\n    Search choices and useful variations:\n    - Base resolution: 360 (parent if available)\n    - Grid factor: 2 (double grid size from each to next)\n    - Optimization method: Projected L-BFGS-B with expertified gradient\n    - Jitter configuration:\n        jitter_range = [0.001, 0.01, 0.1] (optional exploration)\n        Sample jitter^3 per grid size\n    - Diversity check: Maintain historical best scores per grid\n    - Stop conditions:\n        time-based: prune when improvement < 1e-5 per 100 evals\n        feasibility check: maintain normalization \u00b11e-6\n\n    Compute budget and robustness:\n    - Estimated time per grid: 200-400 sec (640 samples)\n    - Timeweighting: allocate budget proportionally by grid size\n    - Recovery strategy: If interrupted between grids, project current candidate to scaled grid\n    - Implementation optimizations:\n        - Use of lag-selective convolution (only compute lags near correlation peak)\n        - Precomputation of shifted patterns for selected lags\n        - Early-termination of subpar optimizations\n        - Resource monitoring to abstract solver-time estimates\n\n    Validation and return contract:\n    - Projection before return: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and \u2211\u2261n/2\n    - Native evaluation: Compute overlap only for \"full\" mode\n    - Fallback: Maintain best candidate found\n    - Raise for exceptional cases: NaN/Inf objective values\n    - Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n    - c5_bound derived from full convolution max",
